========================
AWIPS II Build Container
========================

DEVELOPER SETUP INSTRUCTIONS
1. Install podman if you haven't already
2. Put all git repos in $HOME/builddirs/git
3. Set up build directory structure using setup-build-dirs.sh
4. If you are behind a proxy, add TLS certificates to image-data/certs
5. Build the container image using build-image.sh
6. Set up build variables in env.sh
7. Add build user config to $HOME/builddirs/home-data
8. Start the build using new-awips2-build.sh

TROUBLESHOOTING
1. Disk usage
2. Image build failing

OTHER TOPICS
1. Maven builds


============================
DEVLOPER SETUP INSTRUCTIONS
============================

----------------------------------
Install podman on your workstation
----------------------------------

You need it to run and manage containers and container images:

    $ sudo -E yum install podman

Note: If no podman is found by yum try the following command,
      and then repeat the podman install command:

sudo subscription-manager refresh

Note: You may need to enable workstation extras also and re-run refresh

RHEL7 Laptop:
sudo -E subscription-manager repos --enable=rhel-7-workstation-extras-rpms

RHEL8 Laptop:
sudo -E subscription-manager repos --enable=rhel-8-workstation-extras-rpms

You are assumed to know how to use podman to start and stop containers, build
and delete container images, and execute commands in a running container.

Podman documentation is available here:
http://docs.podman.io/en/latest/index.html

A few useful podman commands.

Podman help:
sudo podman --help
sudo podman container --help
sudo podman image --help

List all podman containers/images:
sudo podman container list -a
sudo podman image list -a

Prune, remove/delete exited/un-used containers/images:
sudo podman container prune
sudo podman image prune

Remove containers/images:
sudo podman container rm [container name/id]
sudo podman image rm [image name/id]

Start podman container:
sudo podman start -ai [container name/id]

Run a command from outside of a container:
sudo podman run imageID ls -al /home/build/

----------------
Set up git repos
----------------

By default, the helper scripts provided will look for your git repos in
$HOME/builddirs/git. Create the directory $HOME/builddirs if it does not exist.
You can symlink $HOME/builddirs/git to the directory where your git repos are
actually located.

At minimum, you need to have the AWIPS2_build and AWIPS2_baseline repos.  If
you just want to build a particular baseline FOSS package (for example:
PostgreSQL or Qpid broker), these two repos will be sufficient.

If you are doing a build of AWIPS that includes any part of CAVE or EDEX, you
need to have at least the following repos in $HOME/builddirs/git:

    AWIPS2_build
    AWIPS2_static
    ufcore
    ufcore-foss
    AWIPS2_foss
    AWIPS2_baseline
    AWIPS2_NCEP


------------------------
Set up build directories
------------------------

Run setup-build-dirs.sh. This script will set up the directory structure used
by the build in $HOME/builddirs. It will also copy the default env.sh into
$HOME/builddirs/home-data. env.sh is sourced inside the container each time you
run the build. It contains environment variables that determine build behavior.


--------------------
Add TLS certificates
--------------------

If you are behind a proxy, copy all TLS certificates for the proxy into
image-data/certs. Otherwise the image build will be unable to access the
internet to download packages.


-------------------------
Build the container image
-------------------------

Run build-image.sh and provide the type of container image you want to build as
the argument to the script. You can run the script without arguments to see
what image types are available. Note that if your workstation does not have a
Red Hat subscription, You will not be able to build Red Hat container images.
You can still build Rocky Linux container images.

You can rerun build-image.sh to rebuild the container image if you make changes
to the Dockerfile.

example:
./build-image.sh rocky8
or
./build-image.sh rhel8


--------------------------------
Set up build variables in env.sh
--------------------------------

Variables in $HOME/builddirs/home-data/env.sh determine which git repos are
included in the build, as well as which RPM packages are produced. You may edit
this file according to the directions in the file, to enable/disable specific
git repos, or to only build a single package or list of packages.

IMPORTANT: Make sure env.sh contains this line:

     export NO_MODIFY_GIT_REPOS=1

Otherwise any uncommitted changes in your git repos may be overwritten when you
start the build.


----------------------------------
Add build user config to home-data
----------------------------------

If you need to add anything to the home directory of the build user (such as
.ssh, .gitconfig, or .m2), copy it into $HOME/builddirs/home-data. Each time
you run the build, the contents of this directory are copied directly into
/home/build inside the container.


---------------
Start the build
---------------

To start a new build container, run ./new-awips2-build.sh followed by the type
of image you want to create the container from. You can run the script without
arguments to see which image types are available. A new container will be
created and a bash shell will start. To start the build, run $HOME/run_build.sh
inside the container. If you exit the bash shell, the container will terminate.
To start the container again, you may run "podman start -ai [container-id]".

Built RPMs are located inside the container in /home/build/workspace/rpmbuild.
To access them from outside the container, you may copy them to either
/home/build/git or /home/build/home-data. There is also the option of modifying
new-awips2-build.sh to your build container with /home/build/workspace as a
mounted volume corresponding to (for example) $HOME/builddirs/workspace outside
the container. Read up on the --volume (or -v) option of the "podman run"
command here:
http://docs.podman.io/en/latest/markdown/podman-run.1.html


===============
TROUBLESHOOTING
===============

----------
Disk usage
----------

Container images and containers can use up a huge amount of disk space. If you
are running podman as a non-root user then this disk space will be used up in
your home directory. Currently, however, we only support running as root, so
this space will be used up on whatever volume contains /var, which is likely
the root volume. This may threaten the stability of your system.

Potential fixes for running out of space:

- Delete unused objects with "podman system prune". Or, if you really want to
  start fresh, you can wipe out all containers and container images with the
  following two commands:

    $ podman rm -af
    $ podman rmi -af

- When running as root, podman stores container data in /var/lib/containers.
  This directory can be many GB in size. If you need to free up space in your
  root volume, and you have plenty of space on a different volume, you may want
  to move that directory to the more spacious volume and make
  /var/lib/containers a symlink to the new location. Don't do this while you
  have any containers running.


-------------------
Image build failing
-------------------

If your image build is failing due to it being unable to connect to the
internet, and you are behind a proxy, the problem is likely proxy-related.
Things to try:

- Make sure you have the following variables correctly set in in your
  environment:

    http_proxy
    https_proxy
    ftp_proxy
    no_proxy

- Make sure image-data/certs contains all the certificates you need to validate
  the proxy.

- Last resort is to get out from behind the proxy to run your image build, if
  you can.

If you are building a RHEL container image and it's failing to download
repomd.xml, you may need to run "subscription-manager refresh" on your
workstation as root.


============
OTHER TOPICS
============

------------
Maven builds
------------

As you may know, Maven looks in $HOME/.m2 for cached packages, and if any
packages it needs aren't there, it tries to download them from the Internet.
If you have to build something that uses Maven, and you are unable to connect
to the Internet from within the container for whatever reason, you need to
provide an .m2 directory containing all the packages needed by Maven, so that
it does not have to access the Internet at all.  You can put that directory in
/home/builddirs/home-data outside the container.  Each time you start the
build, the contents of home-data are rsync'd into /home/build.

